<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/ethics-three-theories">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    What makes an action right? Notice the question. It is not "what do people in my society approve
    of?" or "what does my tradition teach?" — normative ethics asks what <em>justifies</em> a moral
    judgement, and demands an answer that would still stand if you were raised elsewhere. Philosophy's
    three great answers look at three different things. <strong>Consequentialism</strong> looks at
    outcomes: count the good produced. <strong>Deontology</strong> looks at duties and rules: some acts
    are forbidden or required regardless of results. <strong>Virtue ethics</strong> looks at character:
    the right act is what a practically wise, honest, brave person would characteristically do. The
    theories are not merely three recipes producing the same dinners — as we will see, they genuinely
    disagree in hard cases, and each has a famous weakness the others exploit. Most everyday moral
    instincts borrow from all three, which is precisely why knowing them separately is useful: it lets
    you see <em>which</em> consideration is doing the work when you judge.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the ethics conversation, twenty-five centuries long — filter by era" widget="timeline" config={ethTimeline} />

  <h2>Bentham: pleasure counted</h2>
  <p>
    Jeremy Bentham (1789) built the first systematic consequentialism on a bold simplification: the
    only things ultimately good in themselves are pleasure and the absence of pain. Everything else —
    money, friendship, health, knowledge — is good only because it feeds pleasure or blocks pain. This
    is <strong>hedonism</strong>, and combined with the demand to count <em>everyone's</em> pleasure
    equally (each to count for one, nobody for more than one) it becomes <strong>utilitarianism</strong>:
    the right act is the one producing the greatest net happiness for the greatest number. Bentham,
    true to his reforming temperament, made the test mechanical. His <strong>utility (felicific)
    calculus</strong> scores a pleasure along dimensions: intensity, duration, certainty, propinquity
    (how soon), fecundity (will it breed further pleasures?), purity (will it cost pains later?) and
    extent (how many people?).
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked judgement: a city must choose between a park or a car park on one plot. The car park
    pleases 4,000 daily commuters <em>intensely</em> and <em>certainly</em> (propinquity high). The park
    pleases perhaps 40,000 residents weakly, over decades, with high fecundity — walks become habits,
    habits improve health, health compounds. Bentham's dimensions do not just rank the options; they
    expose <em>why</em> we hesitate: the park wins on extent, duration and fecundity, the car park on
    intensity and certainty. His calculus was also radical politics. In 1789, arithmetic pleasure-counting
    of "extent" included the pleasure of animals ("the question is not, Can they reason? nor, Can they
    talk? but, Can they suffer?") and argued against slavery and for decriminalising homosexuality —
    verdicts his contemporaries called monstrous and history calls correct.
  </p>

  <h2>Mill: some pleasures are better</h2>
  <p>
    John Stuart Mill, Bentham's godson, inherited the theory and its embarrassments. A theory that
    counts only <em>quantity</em> of pleasure endorses the "doctrine of swine" — push-pin (a low
    parlour game) is as good as poetry if the pleasure is equal — and it cannot explain why an
    intelligent person's misery can outrank a contented fool's happiness. Mill's fix (1861/1863) was
    <strong>qualitative hedonism</strong>: pleasures differ in kind, not just amount, and pleasures
    exercising our higher faculties (intellect, imagination, moral feeling) are <em>intrinsically
    superior</em>. His test is democratic, not elitist: ask those who have experienced both kinds, and
    they reliably prefer the higher even at the cost of contentment — hence "it is better to be a
    human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied; better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool
    satisfied."
  </p>
  <p>
    Mill also offered a famous <strong>"proof"</strong> of the greatest happiness principle in
    <em>Utilitarianism</em> (1863): the only proof that something is desirable is that people actually
    desire it; everyone desires their own happiness; therefore happiness is desirable — and, he added,
    each person's happiness is a good to <em>that</em> person, so the general happiness is a good to
    the aggregate of persons. Critics instantly flagged the slide: from "everyone desires their own
    happiness" to "everyone <em>ought</em> to promote everyone's happiness" is an inference that
    needs a premise Mill never quite states. Modern utilitarianism largely sidesteps the problem by
    becoming <strong>preference utilitarianism</strong> (associated with Peter Singer): maximise the
    satisfaction of preferences rather than pleasure, which handles the case of the ascetic who
    genuinely prefers less pleasure and more meaning — counting what people want, not what makes them
    smile.
  </p>

  <h2>Act vs rule, and four standing objections</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Act utilitarianism</strong> applies the test case by case: <em>this</em> act, <em>these</em>
    consequences. It is demanding and — critics say — monstrous in the wrong hands. <strong>Rule
    utilitarianism</strong> instead asks: which <em>rules</em>, generally followed, maximise utility?
    Then follow the rules (don't lie, keep promises, don't punish the innocent) even when this once,
    breaking the rule would "pay". It trades a little optimisation for stability and trust, though
    critics press a dilemma: if rules can always be overridden when utility demands, it collapses into
    act utilitarianism; if they can never be overridden, why not justify the rules directly?
  </p>
  <p>
    Four classic issues sharpen the choice. The <strong>tyranny of the majority</strong>: pure
    utility-counting can license trampling a minority — the pleasure of a bigoted crowd outweighed
    the pain of the person they scapegoat, which is why Mill himself needed a separate work
    (<em>On Liberty</em>) to carve out rights that utility alone could not guarantee. The
    <strong>experience machine</strong> (Robert Nozick, 1974): imagine a tank that feeds you flawless
    pleasurable experiences — would you plug in for life? Most refuse, which suggests we value
    <em>contact with reality</em>, not just experience of it — a problem for any theory that makes
    experience the sole good. <strong>Calculation</strong>: real decisions involve consequences we
    cannot forecast, preferences we cannot compare, and no time to run the calculus. And
    <strong>partiality</strong>: utilitarianism counts every person's utility equally, which condemns
    spending your Saturday with your own child rather than volunteering where help is scarcer —
    yet partiality to one's own is arguably part of what a good human life <em>is</em>.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> utilitarianism does not mean "the end justifies any means" or
  "maximise pleasure selfishly". The utility counted is everyone's, impartially, and rule
  utilitarians would say the long-run cost of "any means" thinking is catastrophic. The theory's
  real vulnerability is narrower: it cannot obviously rule out <em>any</em> sacrifice of an
  individual if the numbers come out right.</p>

  <h2>Kant: duty and the good will</h2>
  <p>
    Immanuel Kant (1785) starts from the opposite pole. What makes an action good cannot be its
    consequences — those lie outside our control — but the <strong>good will</strong>: acting from
    duty, out of respect for the moral law, rather than from inclination. The shopkeeper who is
    honest because honesty <em>pays</em> acts correctly but not <em>morally</em>; the one who gives
    fair change because it is right, even to a stranger who would never know, acts from duty.
    Kant distinguishes <strong>hypothetical imperatives</strong> — "if you want X, do Y", binding
    only on those who share the goal (if you want to be healthy, exercise) — from the
    <strong>categorical imperative</strong>: a command binding unconditionally, because it is what
    <em>reason itself</em> requires of any rational agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The categorical imperative has two famous formulations. The <strong>universal law</strong>
    formulation: "act only on that maxim which you can will as a universal law." Test a maxim —
    "make a false promise to get money when it suits me" — by imagining everyone doing it. The
    practice self-destructs: if everyone lied, no one would believe promises, and promise-making
    would be impossible. The maxim cannot even be conceived as universal, so lying is rationally
    incoherent, not merely inconvenient. The <strong>humanity</strong> formulation: "treat humanity,
    whether in yourself or others, always as an end, never merely as a means." Every promise-keeper,
    every manipulator, every exploiter uses other people's rational agency as a tool; the formula
    draws the line between cooperation and use. It grounds human rights with unusual directness —
    each person is an end in themselves, which is exactly what utility-counting struggles to say.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kantian ethics has famous pains too. <strong>Clashing duties</strong>: Kant held lying is always
    wrong, so when a murderer asks where your friend is hiding, you must tell the truth — his
    opponents (and even sympathetic readers like Benjamin Constant, whose letter provoked Kant's
    chilling reply) see this as the theory failing a basic test of moral seriousness.
    <strong>Foot's challenge</strong>: Philippa Foot (1972) argued Kant was wrong that moral demands
    are categorical — they look hypothetical after all ("if you want to be part of a decent human
    community, keep your promises"), binding on us only because we <em>care</em> about what we care
    about. Whether morality is a demand of reason or a contingent shape of caring remains one of the
    field's live disputes.
  </p>

  <h2>Aristotle: what kind of person?</h2>
  <p>
    Virtue ethics (Aristotle, <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, c. 350 BCE) asks the question the other
    two skip: not "which act?" but "which <em>life</em>?" — and answers: the life of
    <strong>eudaimonia</strong>, usually translated "flourishing" or "well-being" but meaning
    something stronger than happiness: living well and doing well, as a human being. Aristotle
    reaches it by the <strong>function argument</strong>: the good of anything that has a function
    lies in performing that function well; what is distinctively human is not growth (plants) or
    perception (animals) but <em>activity of the soul in accordance with reason</em>; therefore human
    good is rational activity, excellently performed, over a whole life. Eudaimonia is the
    <strong>final end</strong> — the one thing we pursue for its own sake and not for anything
    further; money, honour and pleasure are all pursued <em>for</em> something else.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Virtues</strong> are stable excellences of character — courage, honesty, generosity,
    justice, temperance — acquired not by lecture but by habituation: do honest things until honesty
    becomes your default, the way a builder becomes skilled by building. Each virtue sits between two
    vices, the <strong>doctrine of the mean</strong>: courage between cowardice and recklessness,
    generosity between stinginess and wastefulness, proper pride between servility and vanity. The
    mean is not bland moderation; it is <em>relative to us and to the situation</em> — the courage of
    a firefighter entering a fire is an extreme response to ordinary eyes, and exactly right for the
    role. Finding it requires <strong>practical wisdom</strong> (<em>phronesis</em>): the judgement
    that reads a concrete situation, weighs what matters here, and sees the right act — the skill
    every other virtue depends on. Moral responsibility, on this view, attaches to what we do
    <em>voluntarily</em> and with knowledge, and grows with character: we are responsible for our
    virtues because, once adult, we are responsible for the habits that formed them. The classic
    objection is circularity ("right acts are what the virtuous would do" — and who decides?), and
    the standard reply is Aristotle's own anchor: the virtuous person is the one who reliably
    <em>gets eudaimonia-relevant judgement right</em>, whose verdicts survive reflection. Rosalind
    Hursthouse and others have made this framework fully modern.
  </p>

  <h2>One case, three verdicts</h2>
  <p>
    Take lying. A utilitarian asks: does this lie produce more wellbeing than honesty? (Usually no —
    trust is precious — but the Nazi-at-the-door case passes.) A Kantian refuses outright: a lie
    treats the deceived person as a tool, and universalised lying destroys language's point. A virtue
    ethicist asks what the truthful person would do — and finds a third answer: not lying, but also
    not volunteering everything; tact is a virtue too. Three frameworks, three different fault lines:
    consequences, consistency, character.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The sharpest modern battleground is the <strong>trolley problem</strong> (Philippa Foot, 1967,
    developed by Judith Jarvis Thomson): divert a runaway trolley to kill one instead of five? Most
    say divert — utilitarian arithmetic. Then the "footbridge" variant: push a large man off a bridge
    to stop the trolley, same arithmetic, and most refuse — the humanity formula speaks. The gap
    between the two verdicts is where double-effect doctrine, moral luck, and decades of experimental
    philosophy now live. Also deeper: Anscombe's 1958 essay "Modern Moral Philosophy" argued that
    "morally ought" had outlived its divine-law framework and that rule-worship and utility-mania
    both miss something — the paper that revived virtue ethics in the mainstream. And the
    professional takeaway is not to pick a team but to <em>name the framework</em> you are using when
    you judge: most public moral arguments are people in different frameworks shouting past each
    other, and the loudest progress happens when someone says which thing — outcome, duty or
    character — they think is decisive, and why.
  </p>
